# What git is looking at when it looks at the built frontend.
#
# www/build is committed - see the README for why - and almost all of it is
# machine-written output on a single line. Two of git's defaults are wrong for
# that kind of file, so both are turned off:
#
#   -diff   a bundle rendered as a textual diff is one deleted line and one
#           added line of twenty thousand characters. Nobody reads it, and it
#           makes every review of a real change harder to find.
#
#   -merge  a three-way merge of two minified lines can succeed and produce
#           something that is neither side. Unset, git stops and leaves the
#           current branch's version in place with the merge marked as
#           conflicted, which is the honest state: the resolution for a
#           conflict in generated output is to rebuild it, never to edit it.
#
# linguist-generated tells GitHub the same thing, so the files are collapsed in
# a pull request instead of drowning it.
/www/build/** -diff -merge linguist-generated

# The exceptions are the two small, sorted JSON files beside the bundle. They
# are the part of a build a person actually reads during review - which entry
# points exist and which files changed - so they keep an ordinary diff. They
# are still not mergeable by hand: the version of a file is the hash of that
# file, so a merged one would be a version nothing hashes to.
/www/build/.vite/manifest.json diff -linguist-generated
/www/build/.vite/versions.json diff -linguist-generated
